          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Atiyah-Hirzebruch spectral sequence
            (AHSS) is a type of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+sequence"} spectral sequence
            that generalizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+spectral+sequence"} Serre spectral sequence
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\bullet
            to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^\bullet
          %p
            For any (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+homotopy+type"}> finite
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+sequence"} homotopy fiber sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  F &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; P
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; X
                  }
          %p
            then the corresponding
            = succeed "-Atiyah-Hirzebruch" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} cohomology groups
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            and converges to the proper
            = succeed "-cohomology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi E
                    %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_2^{p,q} =
                  H^p(X,E^q(F))
                  \\Rightarrow
                  E^{\bullet}(P)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is of interest already for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \simeq \ast
          #NoteOnTerminology.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong (note on terminology)
            %p
              Often the terminology “Atiyah-Hirzebruch spectral sequence” is taken to refer to only this case with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo =
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = \ast
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#AtiyahHirzebruch61"} Atiyah-Hirzebruch 61,p. 17
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = \ast
              is labeled “Theorem”, while the general case, stated right after the theorem, is labeled “2.2 Remark”.
            %p
              The proof of the theorem that is given is very short, it just says that since topological K-theory satisfies the
              %a{:href => "generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology#ExactnessUnreduced"} exactness axiom
              of a generalized cohomology theory, it is immediate that the conditions for a spectral sequence stated as Axioms (SP.1)-(SP.5) in (
              %a{:href => "#CartanEilenberg56"}> Cartan-Eilenberg 56, section XV.7
              ) are met. Indeed Example 2 in (
              %a{:href => "#CartanEilenberg56"}> Cartan-Eilenberg 56, section XV.7
              ) observes that the spectral sequence in question exists for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              “some fixed
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            %p
              In view of this, the contribution of (
              %a{:href => "#AtiyahHirzebruch61"}> Atiyah-Hirzebruch 61
              ) would not be so much the observation of what is now called the AHSS, rather than the proof that K-theory satisfies the axioms of a generalized cohomology theory. Indeed, according to (
              %a{:href => "#Adams74"}> Adams 74, p. 127-128, 215
              ), the AHSS was earlier observed by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Whitehead"} George Whitehead
              and “then became a folk-theorem” which was “eventually published by Atiyah and Hirzebruch”.
            %p Maybe it should be called the “Cartan-Eilenberg-Whitehead spectral sequence”.
          %p
            There is a generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology+theory"} equivariant cohomology theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#DavisLueck98"}> Davis-Lueck 98, theorem 4.7
            ).
          %p
